ould be a pair. If I can form three chows and a pair of T6s, that might work. Let's see:\n\nChow 1: T4, T5, T6\nChow 2: T5, T6, T7 (but I have one T5, two T6, one T7. If I discard a T5, maybe not. Wait, I have one T5. To form T5, T6, T7, I need T5, T6, T7. I have all three. So that's one chow. Then T4, T5, T6 (but I only have one T5 here). Hmm, maybe overlapping chows aren't allowed. Each tile can only be in one meld. So if I use T5 in T5-T6-T7, I can't use it in T4-T5-T6. Therefore, I need to choose between the two.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form T4-T5-T6 and T5-T6-T7. But that would require two T5s and two T6s. Wait, I have one T5 and two T6s. So that's not possible. Therefore, I can only form one chow here.\n\nLooking at the Dots: B1 is a single tile. B4 and B5. Maybe B4, B5, B6? If I have B4 and B5, I need B6. There are 4 B6s left. If I can get a B6, that would form a chow. But I don't have it. Alternatively, maybe B1 is part of a pair. But I only have one B1.\n\nHonors: F4 and J3. If I can form a pair of F4 or J3, but I only have one each. Unless I discard and draw another.\n\nLooking at the other suited tiles. W3 and W7. Maybe part of a chow in Characters? W3 could be part of 3-4-5, but I don't have W4 or W5. W7 could be part of 7-8-9, but I don't have W8 or W9. So maybe not helpful.\n\nSo possible options for melds:\n\n1. T4-T5-T6 (chow)\n2. T5-T6-T7 (chow)\n3. B4-B5-B6 (if I get B6)\n4. Maybe a pung of T6 if I get another T6 (but only 2 left, and I have two already)\n5. Pair of T6s (since I have two)\n\nIf I can form two chows and a pair of T6s, then I need two more melds. The other tiles are W3, W7, B1, B9, F4, J3. Maybe W3 and W7 could be part of a pung if I have more, but I only have one each. B1 and B9 are terminals. Maybe B1 is part of a pair with another B1, but I don't have it. B9 is a single tile.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the B1 is part of a pair with another B1. But again, no. So perhaps I need to form another chow or pung.\n\nLooking at the B4 a