nts). But I need to form the chows.\n\nBut since I can only discard a tile now, maybe the best is to set up for forming a chow. For example, if I discard T7, then I have T4, T5, T6 left. If someone discards a T7, I can chi it? Wait, no. Because to chi, I need to have two tiles and take the third from the discard. If I discard T7, then I have T4, T5, T6. If someone discards a T7, I can't chi because I need to have two tiles in my hand. Wait, if I have T4, T5, T6, and someone discards T7, I can't form a chow with T6 and T7. Because a chow is three consecutive. If I have T4, T5, T6, and someone discards T7, I can't form a chow. Alternatively, if I have T5, T6, and someone discards T4 or T7, I can chi. But if I discard T7, then I have T4, T5, T6. If someone discards T3 or T7, but I don't have those. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T4, then I have T5, T6, T7. If someone discards T4, I can chi T4 to form T4, T5, T6. But I have T5 and T6. Wait, if I have T5 and T6, and someone discards T4, I can chi T4 to form T4, T5, T6. Similarly, if I have T5 and T6, and someone discards T7, I can't form a chow. So maybe discarding T4 would leave me with T5, T6, T7, which could form a chow if I have T5 and T6 and someone discards T7. Wait, no, a chow needs three consecutive. If I have T5 and T6, and someone discards T4 or T7, I can chi. But if I discard T4, then I have T5, T6, T7. If someone discards T4, I can chi T4 to form T4, T5, T6. If someone discards T8, I can't. So maybe discarding T4 is better for setting up a chi.\n\nBut the valid actions include Play T4, which would discard it. So if I discard T4, then I can potentially chi a T4 if someone else discards it. Wait, but I already have T5 and T6. If someone discards T4, I can chi T4 to form T4, T5, T6. That's a chow. Similarly, if I discard T7, then if someone discards T7, I can chi T7 to form T5, T6, T7. But I have T5 and T6. So either way, discarding T4 or T7 could set up a chi.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T5, then I have