re common. Alternatively, maybe T7 is less common. The unshown T4 has 3 left, T7 has 2. So T4 is more available. But others might have already used some. The game history shows that the next player played T4, and the previous player played T4. So maybe T4 is in play. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, looking at the other tiles in my hand. I have two F4s. F4 is North Wind, which is the prevailing wind. The prevailing wind is North, so if I have a pung of F4, that would give me a Prevalent Wind (2 points). But I have two F4s. If I discard one, there are 4 unshown. So if someone else has a F4, they could peng. But I have two, so maybe it's better to keep them in case I can form a pung later. Discarding F4 would leave me with one, which isn't enough for a pung. So maybe it's better not to discard F4 unless necessary.\n\nSimilarly, J3 is White Dragon. If I discard J3, there are 4 unshown. So someone could have a pung. But I have one, so maybe it's safe.\n\nAnother angle: which tile is least needed by others. For example, W8: there are 2 unshown. If I discard W8, maybe someone is after it. But I have one. Discarding it would leave 2. Maybe it's safer. Or maybe not. It's hard to tell.\n\nLooking at the melds of others: the player across has a pung of F2 and a chow of W1-W2-W3. The previous player has a pung of T8 and a chow of W3-W4-W5. So they might be after W3s or T8s. Since I have two W3s, maybe they are after W3s. If I discard a W3, they might take it. But since they already have a chow of W3-W4-W5, they might not need another W3. So maybe it's safe to discard W3. But I have two, so discarding one leaves me with one. Not enough for a pung. So maybe it's better to keep both.\n\nSo considering all this, the best options are to discard T4 or T7 to set up for a chi. Between those, maybe T4 is better because there are more left. So I'll go with discarding T4. That way, if someone discards a T4, I can form a chow of T4-T5-T6. Alternatively, if I discard T7, I can form T5-T6-T7. Either 